01:37:35.333 --> 01:37:36.709
You want what you want.

2506
01:37:36.709 --> 01:37:37.974
Yeah, no, no, except that there's all kinds

2507
01:37:37.974 --> 01:37:38.807
of interesting ways to do that.

2508
01:37:38.807 --> 01:37:40.967
So one, there's an image that I really like

2509
01:37:40.967 --> 01:37:44.760
that's really helpful that a guy, a philosopher of art

2510
01:37:44.760 --> 01:37:47.500
in China taught me, and I was actually touring

2511
01:37:47.500 --> 01:37:49.154
the National Palace Museum in Taipei,

2512
01:37:49.154 --> 01:37:53.170
and we were looking at this sculpture of this chipped,

2513
01:37:53.170 --> 01:37:55.840
you know, piece of jade that, and it was sculptured

2514
01:37:55.840 --> 01:37:56.940
to a Chinese village, and I said,

2515
01:37:56.940 --> 01:37:59.930
look, even if you didn't tell me it was Chinese,

2516
01:37:59.930 --> 01:38:00.763
I would know it was Chinese.

2517
01:38:00.763 --> 01:38:02.595
How do I know just by looking at it it's Chinese,

2518
01:38:02.595 --> 01:38:04.636
you know, if it didn't have a pagoda in it,

2519
01:38:04.636 --> 01:38:06.300
but it's just a, you know, it's just, it looks Chinese.

2520
01:38:06.300 --> 01:38:08.020
He says why, he says, because it's

2521
01:38:08.020 --> 01:38:09.633
a different concept of what art is.

2522
01:38:09.633 --> 01:38:14.200
He said, you think of art as starting with an empty canvas

2523
01:38:14.200 --> 01:38:15.760
and being filled up with brushstrokes.

2524
01:38:15.760 --> 01:38:17.900
We think of art as already existing inside

2525
01:38:17.900 --> 01:38:19.510
the block of jade, which we have to chip away

2526
01:38:19.510 --> 01:38:20.937
until we find the work of art.

2527
01:38:20.937 --> 01:38:23.898
He said, this is also how you see success in the west

2528
01:38:23.898 --> 01:38:26.370
as starting from nothing and building up,

2529
01:38:26.370 --> 01:38:28.880
and then after a while, it's so full that you actually

2530
01:38:28.880 --> 01:38:30.400
can't get any more satisfaction because you,

2531
01:38:30.400 --> 01:38:31.490
it's just more brushstrokes.

2532
01:38:31.490 --> 01:38:33.296
You know, if you've got a completely full canvas,

2533
01:38:33.296 --> 01:38:36.040
effectively, metaphorically, and you're like,

2534
01:38:36.040 --> 01:38:37.100
I don't know, I'm not satisfied.

2535
01:38:37.100 --> 01:38:38.060
Maybe I'll get a boat.

2536
01:38:38.060 --> 01:38:40.990
It's not gonna work because the canvas is already full.

2537
01:38:40.990 --> 01:38:43.260
To find real satisfaction in the second half of life,

2538
01:38:43.260 --> 01:38:46.143
this is a second curve phenomenon, this is one of the things

2539
01:38:46.143 --> 01:38:48.180
that all happy older people have in common,

2540
01:38:48.180 --> 01:38:50.570
they've stopped adding and they start taking away.

2541
01:38:50.570 --> 01:38:54.800
They start chipping away, and each year I throw away

2542
01:38:54.800 --> 01:38:56.190
more stuff until I can find me,

2543
01:38:56.190 --> 01:38:58.010
which is intensely satisfying.

2544
01:38:58.010 --> 01:38:59.740
So the key thing is, what are you gonna

2545
01:38:59.740 --> 01:39:00.951
throw away this year?

2546
01:39:00.951 --> 01:39:05.010
Don't have a bucket list, have a reverse bucket list.

2547
01:39:05.010 --> 01:39:07.493
The reverse bucket list is the list of your attachments

2548
01:39:07.493 --> 01:39:10.849
and your strategic plan for look, if I get it, I get it,

2549
01:39:10.849 --> 01:39:13.531
but I no longer am going to consider myself a loser

2550
01:39:13.531 --> 01:39:16.370
if I don't, you know, ride in a hot air balloon

2551
01:39:16.370 --> 01:39:18.233
or you know, have lunch with the President.

2552
01:39:18.233 --> 01:39:20.363
I am detaching myself officially, and that

2553
01:39:20.363 --> 01:39:23.591
metacognitive exercise really, really works, and I do that.

2554
01:39:23.591 --> 01:39:25.170
You know, one year I gave away

2555
01:39:25.170 --> 01:39:27.040
two thirds of my clothes.
Simply by making a,

2556
01:39:27.040 --> 01:39:28.930
like making a decision to do it?

2557
01:39:28.930 --> 01:39:30.970
Making a decision to be detached and then act accordingly,

2558
01:39:30.970 --> 01:39:32.553
I gave away two thirds of my clothes.

2559
01:39:32.553 --> 01:39:35.190
Last year or the year before on my birthday,

2560
01:39:35.190 --> 01:39:37.273
I gave away half of my political opinions.

2561
01:39:37.273 --> 01:39:39.320
I wrote down all my political opinions.

2562
01:39:39.320 --> 01:39:40.800
I detached myself from half of them.

2563
01:39:40.800 --> 01:39:42.347
That's so interesting, well,

2564
01:39:42.347 --> 01:39:43.833
for two things, first of all.
It's just, it's hardcore.

2565
01:39:43.833 --> 01:39:45.875
You're kind of a clotheshorse, right?

2566
01:39:45.875 --> 01:39:47.571
Like, you're a sartorial maven.

2567
01:39:47.571 --> 01:39:51.030
So giving away your clothes was,

2568
01:39:51.030 --> 01:39:53.260
probably there might have been a little pinch there.

2569
01:39:53.260 --> 01:39:55.290
I might have not given away the best ones but.

2570
01:39:55.290 --> 01:39:56.300
Okay.

2571
01:39:56.300 --> 01:39:59.850
All right, fair enough, and it is interesting

2572
01:39:59.850 --> 01:40:02.160
because you were once known and have written

2573
01:40:02.160 --> 01:40:04.353
many books about politics.

2574
01:40:04.353 --> 01:40:05.352
Yeah.

2575
01:40:05.352 --> 01:40:07.530
But if you kind of go online and research everything

2576
01:40:07.530 --> 01:40:10.090
you've been talking about, there's none of that.

2577
01:40:10.090 --> 01:40:12.550
Like, you've really kind of put that aside

2578
01:40:12.550 --> 01:40:14.371
and said, I'm focused on this now.

2579
01:40:14.371 --> 01:40:17.140
That is, and it is pursuant to the decision

2580
01:40:17.140 --> 01:40:20.780
in my reverse bucket list birthday exercise to detach myself

2581
01:40:20.780 --> 01:40:22.470
more consciously from something that had been

2582
01:40:22.470 --> 01:40:24.110
quite important to me, quite frankly,

2583
01:40:24.110 --> 01:40:25.800
but was holding me back.

2584
01:40:25.800 --> 01:40:27.970
It was holding me back from bonds of love with people

2585
01:40:27.970 --> 01:40:28.803
who are different than me.

2586
01:40:28.803 --> 01:40:30.540
Are you happier you're not

2587
01:40:30.540 --> 01:40:31.834
part of that conversation?
Yeah.

2588
01:40:31.834 --> 01:40:33.314
Hell yeah, I really am, and again,

2589
01:40:33.314 --> 01:40:35.370
I've got nothing against the conversation.

2590
01:40:35.370 --> 01:40:38.130
I want a responsible country that has good public policy

2591
01:40:38.130 --> 01:40:40.246
and better politics, but I actually don't think

2592
01:40:40.246 --> 01:40:42.550
I have an oracle on truth.

2593
01:40:42.550 --> 01:40:45.052
I know I'm wrong, I just don't know on what,

2594
01:40:45.052 --> 01:40:48.340
and actually getting away from intense opinions

2595
01:40:48.340 --> 01:40:50.733
and big arguments has been a kind of detachment

2596
01:40:50.733 --> 01:40:52.690
that has truly set me free.

2597
01:40:52.690 --> 01:40:53.813
It set me free to.
Do you have to

2598
01:40:53.813 --> 01:40:55.390
just stay off Twitter?

2599
01:40:55.390 --> 01:40:56.389
Well, I mean, I can stay.
I mean, that's gotta be

2600
01:40:56.389 --> 01:40:59.520
provocative when you see a bunch of craziness.

2601
01:40:59.520 --> 01:41:00.353
Yeah, yeah, but it's actually.

2602
01:41:00.353 --> 01:41:01.888
And you have this urge to jump in and.

2603
01:41:01.888 --> 01:41:03.027
(Arthur laughing)

2604
01:41:03.027 --> 01:41:04.034
Right.

2605
01:41:04.034 --> 01:41:06.530
Well, since I made the detachment decision about my,

2606
01:41:06.530 --> 01:41:08.690
about political opinions, I get a lot more,

2607
01:41:08.690 --> 01:41:12.360
I get more laughs from people, you know, who are,

2608
01:41:12.360 --> 01:41:14.140
you know, saying outlandish things, I have to say.

2609
01:41:14.140 --> 01:41:15.710
I mean, look, I'm alarmed about what's happening

2610
01:41:15.710 --> 01:41:17.780
in this country, and I believe we need to love

2611
01:41:17.780 --> 01:41:20.120
our enemies politically a lot more than we do.

2612
01:41:20.120 --> 01:41:22.350
That's super important for us to do, and I'm just

2613
01:41:22.350 --> 01:41:25.650
better able to do it, and to model that, to build alliances

2614
01:41:25.650 --> 01:41:28.110
with people who would ordinarily be very, very different

2615
01:41:28.110 --> 01:41:30.205
than me politically when I'm not attached.

2616
01:41:30.205 --> 01:41:32.355
I mean, look, I got these opinions, but I can take 'em

2617
01:41:32.355 --> 01:41:34.690
or leave 'em is the whole point.

2618
01:41:34.690 --> 01:41:37.117
It's a, it's interesting, and you know, Thich Nhat Hanh,

2619
01:41:37.117 --> 01:41:39.550
you know, the great Vietnamese Buddhist master,

2620
01:41:39.550 --> 01:41:41.490
he said the greatest attachments that many have

2621
01:41:41.490 --> 01:41:42.765
is to their opinions.

2622
01:41:42.765 --> 01:41:45.877
That had a big effect on me 'cause we never think of that.

2623
01:41:45.877 --> 01:41:48.910
It's like, yeah, it's my material possessions, and.

2624
01:41:48.910 --> 01:41:50.340
Well, there's an identity construct in that.

2625
01:41:50.340 --> 01:41:51.700
Yeah, for sure.

2626
01:41:51.700 --> 01:41:53.957
Absolutely, for sure and by the way, give away parts

2627
01:41:53.957 --> 01:41:57.109
of your identity, give away your concept of who you are,

2628
01:41:57.109 --> 01:41:59.620
detach yourself from these things about who

2629
01:41:59.620 --> 01:42:02.480
you actually are, and that's been really helpful

2630
01:42:02.480 --> 01:42:04.040
to me too, I have to say.

2631
01:42:04.040 --> 01:42:04.894
That's very liberating.

2632
01:42:04.894 --> 01:42:06.080
Hugely.

2633
01:42:06.080 --> 01:42:10.490
It's actually one of the greatest secrets that I've ever

2634
01:42:10.490 --> 01:42:12.208
been able to come across is the reverse bucket list

2635
01:42:12.208 --> 01:42:15.128
around ideology, identity and opinions.

2636
01:42:15.128 --> 01:42:18.530
Yeah, it's sort of, it's a PhD in minimalism

2637
01:42:18.530 --> 01:42:21.640
because it's really a mindful practice.

2638
01:42:21.640 --> 01:42:24.260
It's not about, I mean, cleaning out your closet

2639
01:42:24.260 --> 01:42:26.512
is an activity, but it's not really about

2640
01:42:26.512 --> 01:42:28.823
getting rid of your possessions.

2641
01:42:28.823 --> 01:42:32.860
It's about clarity of purpose and thought, and removing

2642
01:42:32.860 --> 01:42:35.940
the clutter of your conscious experience

2643
01:42:35.940 --> 01:42:38.260
and the traps of your identity that are getting

2644
01:42:38.260 --> 01:42:41.890
in the way of you being a more fulfilled human.

2645
01:42:41.890 --> 01:42:43.460
Yeah, and when you make a commitment to it

2646
01:42:43.460 --> 01:42:45.047
and somebody says something that you would've snapped at

2647
01:42:45.047 --> 01:42:47.363
before, you're more likely to go, huh.

2648
01:42:47.363 --> 01:42:48.880
Right.

2649
01:42:48.880 --> 01:42:49.713
Huh.

2650
01:42:49.713 --> 01:42:50.801
Tell me more about that.

2651
01:42:50.801 --> 01:42:52.827
Come sit next to me. (men laughing)

2652
01:42:52.827 --> 01:42:57.827
So, when you go back to the think tank and walk the halls,

2653
01:42:58.080 --> 01:43:01.968
can you like, it must be, you know, like, did they,

2654
01:43:01.968 --> 01:43:04.689
like your old colleagues, are they confused?

2655
01:43:04.689 --> 01:43:06.125
I don't know.

2656
01:43:06.125 --> 01:43:07.126
You don't know?

2657
01:43:07.126 --> 01:43:08.131
I don't really know.

2658
01:43:08.131 --> 01:43:08.964
You transcended that.

2659
01:43:08.964 --> 01:43:11.250
Well, I mean, I've gone back a couple of times

2660
01:43:11.250 --> 01:43:13.173
and like, I love these people and I think

2661
01:43:13.173 --> 01:43:15.563
they're doing absolutely outstanding work.

2662
01:43:15.563 --> 01:43:16.750
I just love the work they do.

2663
01:43:16.750 --> 01:43:18.608
I'm so proud to have been part of that,

2664
01:43:18.608 --> 01:43:23.608
but I also recognize that I did go poof and it's okay.

2665
01:43:24.040 --> 01:43:25.910
It's absolutely okay, and I'll talk to people

2666
01:43:25.910 --> 01:43:28.730
and they'll kind of be like, what happened to you, man?

2667
01:43:28.730 --> 01:43:30.153
It's interesting 'cause I used to be,

2668
01:43:30.153 --> 01:43:32.120
I was a musician for so many years.

2669
01:43:32.120 --> 01:43:33.150
Yeah, we didn't even talk about that.

2670
01:43:33.150 --> 01:43:35.232
Yeah, and I was a classical musician, but I also toured

2671
01:43:35.232 --> 01:43:38.420
for two years with a jazz guitar player, Charlie Byrd.

2672
01:43:38.420 --> 01:43:39.253
Oh, you did?

2673
01:43:39.253 --> 01:43:41.360
Yeah, yeah, and I was on tour with Charlie Byrd

2674
01:43:41.360 --> 01:43:44.587
all over the place and years ago,

2675
01:43:44.587 --> 01:43:47.420
probably six or seven years ago, I was doing this,

2676
01:43:47.420 --> 01:43:49.762
I was hosting this big event in Washington, DC.

2677
01:43:49.762 --> 01:43:52.287
You know, 2,000 people, black tie at

2678
01:43:52.287 --> 01:43:54.900
the National Building Museum, you know, and we had

2679
01:43:54.900 --> 01:43:57.520
this big event and Benjamin Netanyahu was

2680
01:43:57.520 --> 01:44:00.260
this guest speaker, and it was a huge thing.

2681
01:44:00.260 --> 01:44:02.710
The press corps was all there and we had protesters outside.

2682
01:44:02.710 --> 01:44:04.634
It was a great night, it was a fantastic fun night,

2683
01:44:04.634 --> 01:44:07.437
and we hired a band and the, you know, the band was playing

2684
01:44:07.437 --> 01:44:09.080
and they were pretty good, and at the end, you know,

2685
01:44:09.080 --> 01:44:10.270
we're kind of cleaning up and you know,

2686
01:44:10.270 --> 01:44:11.707
I was the president of the organization.

2687
01:44:11.707 --> 01:44:13.893
I'd given the remarks and one of the keynote addresses

2688
01:44:13.893 --> 01:44:16.958
and afterward, you know, my my bow tie is untied

2689
01:44:16.958 --> 01:44:18.730
and it's 11 o'clock at night.

2690
01:44:18.730 --> 01:44:20.900
I'm pretty satisfied, and I noticed the drummer

2691
01:44:20.900 --> 01:44:22.050
is coming down from the bandstand.

2692
01:44:22.050 --> 01:44:23.400
He's walking over to me and this, you know,

2693
01:44:23.400 --> 01:44:24.240
the place is clearing out.

2694
01:44:24.240 --> 01:44:27.459
He says, are you Arthur Brooks, the French horn player,

2695
01:44:27.459 --> 01:44:29.740
'cause that's what I did for a living,

2696
01:44:29.740 --> 01:44:30.960
and I recognized him.

2697
01:44:30.960 --> 01:44:33.798
He was the drummer for Charlie Byrd and I'd made two records

2698
01:44:33.798 --> 01:44:37.730
with him 20 years earlier, 25 years earlier,

2699
01:44:37.730 --> 01:44:40.178
and he's looking at me and he says,

2700
01:44:40.178 --> 01:44:43.255
are you the boss of all this now?

2701
01:44:43.255 --> 01:44:44.474
I said, yeah, yeah, I am.

2702
01:44:44.474 --> 01:44:47.237
He says, what happened to you, man?

2703
01:44:47.237 --> 01:44:48.070
(men laughing)

2704
01:44:48.070 --> 01:44:49.514
It's the same thing, right?

2705
01:44:49.514 --> 01:44:50.347
It's the same thing.

2706
01:44:50.347 --> 01:44:52.456
It's a crazy story to be this French horn prodigy

2707
01:44:52.456 --> 01:44:55.420
and to have this whole career throughout your 20s

2708
01:44:55.420 --> 01:44:59.056
and be playing in these orchestras and really be being,

2709
01:44:59.056 --> 01:45:04.056
you know, in pursuit of this career of mastery of music

2710
01:45:04.176 --> 01:45:06.950
and then to discover in your 20s that you just

2711
01:45:06.950 --> 01:45:09.031
couldn't play as well as you once could,

2712
01:45:09.031 --> 01:45:12.050
and the struggle to try to figure out how to solve it

2713
01:45:12.050 --> 01:45:14.996
until ultimately you just, you have to give it up.

2714
01:45:14.996 --> 01:45:16.150
Yeah, yeah, I gave it up.

2715
01:45:16.150 --> 01:45:18.770
And then it bursts this whole other career.

2716
01:45:18.770 --> 01:45:21.530
It reminds me of, do you know Maya Shankar?

2717
01:45:21.530 --> 01:45:22.363
Yeah.

2718
01:45:22.363 --> 01:45:24.200
It's a similar, very similar story.

2719
01:45:24.200 --> 01:45:25.416
Yeah, yeah, yeah, the violinist?

2720
01:45:25.416 --> 01:45:27.050
Yes, yeah, yeah, yeah.

2721
01:45:27.050 --> 01:45:30.293
It's, playing a classical instrument is hugely

2722
01:45:30.293 --> 01:45:35.293
coordination and strength endeavor,

2723
01:45:35.930 --> 01:45:36.980
strength-oriented endeavor.

2724
01:45:36.980 --> 01:45:39.450
It's like being an athlete, but with fine motor skills

2725
01:45:39.450 --> 01:45:41.960
as opposed to gross motor skills, and there's a lot

2726
01:45:41.960 --> 01:45:43.960
of things that can go wrong, and there's a lot of things

2727
01:45:43.960 --> 01:45:46.070
that we don't understand that can actually go wrong.

2728
01:45:46.070 --> 01:45:47.700
There can be repetitive stress injuries.

2729
01:45:47.700 --> 01:45:48.650
There can be nerve damage.

2730
01:45:48.650 --> 01:45:51.390
There can be actually muscle tears at the finest level.

2731
01:45:51.390 --> 01:45:53.420
You can be a French horn player like I am

2732
01:45:53.420 --> 01:45:56.078
and tear a tiny muscle in your upper lip, and you're cooked.

2733
01:45:56.078 --> 01:45:58.220
I mean, you just, you'll be, you'll go into decline.

2734
01:45:58.220 --> 01:46:00.240
You'll be able to play, but you'll go into decline

2735
01:46:00.240 --> 01:46:03.030
and who knows why, but in my 20s I was getting better.

2736
01:46:03.030 --> 01:46:04.000
Man, I was getting better.

2737
01:46:04.000 --> 01:46:07.140
I was on this upward trajectory and I frankly wanted

2738
01:46:07.140 --> 01:46:08.730
to be the greatest French horn player in the world,

2739
01:46:08.730 --> 01:46:10.170
and it's like, what a great country.

2740
01:46:10.170 --> 01:46:12.100
You can have an ambition, like it's so crazy,

2741
01:46:12.100 --> 01:46:15.990
and things looked good, but when I was 21 or 22 years old,

2742
01:46:15.990 --> 01:46:19.180
I started to get worse and I couldn't quite figure it out,

2743
01:46:19.180 --> 01:46:22.160
and, you know, then I played for a bunch of seasons

2744
01:46:22.160 --> 01:46:26.190
in the Barcelona symphony and I was getting worse.

2745
01:46:26.190 --> 01:46:27.693
I mean, no matter what I, it's, nothing worked

2746
01:46:27.693 --> 01:46:29.790
and I went to the greatest teachers.

2747
01:46:29.790 --> 01:46:32.880
I, and it was like this master class

2748
01:46:32.880 --> 01:46:35.560
in understanding decline, which is very helpful to me,

2749
01:46:35.560 --> 01:46:37.660
I have to say, because, you know, declines that came later

2750
01:46:37.660 --> 01:46:39.720
because of fluid intelligence, not because of

